# G067 — Chat Application (Threading)

*v2. See `chat-app.md` for prose: every actor both producer and
consumer, broadcast as fan-out on every send, dynamic membership
under a single lock, total ordering via monotonic counter, log and
inboxes as journal-vs-queue split.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Agent-to-Agent Broadcast

[research-coordinator @queries
    IN ORDER TO
        coordinate a literature reviewer and a code analyser as peers
        where every agent sees every send in the same total order
        and the coordinator can audit the full conversation via the log

    @room <- [chat[new-room]]
    @me   <- [chat[join {room: @room, name: "Coordinator"}]]

    concurrent [
        literature-reviewer:
            @id <- [chat[join
                           {room: @room, name: "LiteratureReviewer"}]]
            for @q in @queries
                @findings <- [search[papers {query: @q}]]
                [chat[send
                       {room: @room, sender: @id,
                        text: @findings:summary}]]
            [chat[leave {room: @room, id: @id}]]

        code-analyser:
            @id <- [chat[join
                           {room: @room, name: "CodeAnalyser"}]]
            for @q in @queries
                @analysis <- [code[analyse {query: @q}]]
                [chat[send
                       {room: @room, sender: @id, text: @analysis}]]
            [chat[leave {room: @room, id: @id}]]

        coordinator-read-loop:
            every 100 ms
                @msgs <- [chat[drain-inbox {room: @room, id: @me}]]
                for @m in @msgs
                    [ui[render-incoming {msg: @m}]]
    ]
    join

    -> [chat[log {room: @room}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[chat
    new-room
    join            {room, name}                 ;; -> user-id
    leave           {room, id}
    send            {room, sender, text}           ;; broadcast to all
    drain-inbox     {room, id}                     ;; drain-on-read
    log             {room}                          ;; full audit history
    members         {room}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: send atomic `where` membership and counter align

[chat[send {room: @r, sender: @s, text: @t}]
    IN ORDER TO
        fan out to every current member's inbox under one lock
        while assigning a strictly-increasing sequence number
        so every recipient sees messages in the same total order

    acquire @r:lock
        @seq <- ++@r:counter
        @msg <- {seq: @seq, sender: @s, text: @t, ts: now}
        append @msg to @r:log                   ;; journal
        for @member in @r:members
            append @msg to @member:inbox        ;; queue
    release @r:lock
]

Under one lock, the membership snapshot and the counter increment
happen together. Bob-leaves-then-Alice-sends and Alice-sends-then-
Bob-leaves produce different outcomes but neither has a half-state.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: journal vs queue as two `where` different questions

[chat[log {room: @r}]
    returns the append-only history
    -> answers "what did the room say?"

[chat[drain-inbox {room: @r, id: @u}]
    empties @u's unread queue
    -> answers "what do I need to look at?"

Same send populates both. Log survives join and leave. Inbox is per-
user and transient. Both are maintained atomically inside send.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @r <- [chat[new-room]]
    @alice <- [chat[join {room: @r, name: "alice"}]]
    @bob   <- [chat[join {room: @r, name: "bob"}]]

    [chat[send {room: @r, sender: @alice, text: "hi"}]]
    [chat[send {room: @r, sender: @bob,   text: "hello"}]]

    -> [chat[drain-inbox {room: @r, id: @bob}]]
    ;; -> [{seq: 0, sender: alice, text: "hi"},
    ;;     {seq: 1, sender: bob,   text: "hello"}]

    [chat[leave {room: @r, id: @bob}]]
    [chat[send  {room: @r, sender: @alice, text: "bye"}]]

    -> [chat[drain-inbox {room: @r, id: @alice}]]
    ;; -> [{seq: 2, sender: alice, text: "bye"}]  (bob not in membership)

    -> [chat[log {room: @r}]] count               ;; -> 3 (journal retains all)
]
